Question: do pinned author CTT/RFF and Cheap Permutation code return the biased squared distance between group empirical mean embeddings when group sizes differ? Does CTT's use of raw bin size rather than compressed size also change scale? Could either discrepancy affect permutation rankings, rather than just positive common scale?

Native sources: microsoft/goodpoints 181b12ade5dd02a61b65ed5cb9444c916fa3135f and microsoft/cheap-permutations 8c775da7dc317cbdb84ed768ce93fda55a9e2f2f, MIT. Build unchanged sources with single-thread CPU, record full source inventories and environment. Download/build is a milestone, not a research cycle. Avoid altering installed shared dependencies: isolated .deps, pinned Cython if needed. Runtime source remains unchanged.

Hypothesis to challenge: unequal-count supported APIs compute standard biased MMD squared. Predicted falsifier: identical Gaussian points with one versus three equal-sized bins yield nonzero native CTT, though MMD is zero; common-count control is zero. RFF compared against group feature means generated by the exact same native feature map. Since RFF diagonal varies, identical-point native RFF value depends on the feature vector norm.

Controls: balanced/unbalanced identical inputs; balanced/unbalanced deterministic distinct scalar points with native Gaussian kernel; compressing and identity/no-compression settings with actual selected indices captured by a transparent wrapper that calls the original compression unchanged. Compare returned original statistic to independent dense Gaussian calculation on actual coresets, and bin-signed raw-size-normalized formula. Relative/absolute tolerance 1e-10 for finite float64 small cases. Retain every permutation score and native count/coreset details. Exhaust 15 allocations of 2 out of 6 scalar points [0,.1,.2,1,3,4] (no compression) to test ranking equivalence; report whatever occurs, no search for more favorable fixtures. Standard MMD uses 1/a,-1/b weights; rank disagreements are diagnostic, not false-positive/power evidence.

Boundaries: fixed-count permutations of any statistic can be null-valid with exchangeable summaries. A nonzero constant statistic on identical points does not show level inflation. Multiplication by a fixed positive factor cannot affect permutation ordering; balanced source experiment conclusions are not refuted. Cheap/RFF formula overlap is not novelty. Bandwidth is fixed at 1 for constructed diagnostics, eliminating tuning ambiguity. Native benchmark and fitted-bandwidth audit remain required before broad performance/validity claims.

